# Make or buy?
voices: {"a":"gemini:Achird | curious, open-minded economist host who loves a puzzle; warm, thinks aloud, brisk lively pace (never slow, never rushed); takes objections seriously, never preachy","b":"gemini:Kore | sharp, friendly co-host: asks what a smart listener would, pushes back with real objections, sometimes explains; dry wit, never exaggerated; brisk lively pace, never slow"}
prompt: "What are transaction costs, and what is hold-up? What really happened between General Motors and Fisher Body?"
next: {"title":"How does a firm turn inputs into output?","href":"https://www.drawcast.app/#gh=hmelberg/drawcast-library/econ-from-zero/108-how-does-a-firm-turn-inputs-into-output.cast"}

## A deal cut short
use: timeline
with:
    view:
      from: 1915
      to: 1932
    box:
      x: 20
      'y': 370
      w: 960
      h: 290
    events:
      - {id: buy60, date: 1919, label: GM buys 60%, sublabel: of Fisher Body, priority: 1, cites: [kca1978]}
      - {id: rest, date: 1926, label: GM buys the rest, priority: 1, emphasize: true, cites: [kca1978]}
      - {id: end, date: 1929, label: Deal would end, priority: 1}
    eras:
      - {from: 1919, to: 1929, label: 10-year deal}
      - {from: 1926, to: 1929, label: 3 years early, level: 1, color: '#b5482e'}
adjust:
    event_buy60:
      scale: 1.3
    event_rest:
      scale: 1.3
    event_end:
      scale: 1.3
sources:
    - id: kca1978
      title: Vertical Integration, Appropriable Rents, and the Competitive Contracting Process
      authors: Benjamin Klein, Robert G. Crawford, Armen A. Alchian
      year: 1978
      finding: 'Journal of Law and Economics 21(2): GM bought 60% of Fisher Body in 1919 under a ten-year contract to buy substantially all its closed bodies from Fisher at variable cost plus 17.6%; GM bought the remaining 40% in 1926.'
    - id: williamson1985
      title: The Economic Institutions of Capitalism
      authors: Oliver E. Williamson
      year: 1985
      finding: 'Transaction cost economics: the costs of arranging, writing and enforcing exchanges, worst where assets are specific to one relationship; Nobel prize 2009 for his analysis of economic governance, especially the boundaries of the firm.'

    text formula "price = cost + 17.6%" cites ["kca1978"] x 420 y 270 font_size 44 hidden true
    text coase "using a market costs something" #8f887c x 420 y 270 font_size 36 hidden true
    text tc "transaction costs" #2f6b8f cites ["williamson1985"] x 420 y 290 font_size 44 hidden true
    text tcwords "search · haggle · write · police" x 420 y 225 font_size 36 hidden true
    text sunk "worst: after the deal, money sunk" #b5482e x 420 y 170 font_size 32 hidden true
    portrait williamson x 865 y 235 width 190 of "Oliver E. Williamson" cameo true hidden true
    text r1 "a game →" x 250 y 250 font_size 40 hidden true
    text r2 "the story →" x 500 y 250 font_size 40 hidden true
    text r3 "the doubts" #b5482e x 750 y 250 font_size 40 hidden true

    card title "Make or buy?"

Make or buy? Every firm keeps asking it, and in 1919 General Motors asked it abo (@draw event_buy60@)ut the bodies of its cars.
    draw axis

B: That year GM bou (@highlight event_buy60 effect box@)ght sixty percent of Fisher Body, a maker of closed car bodies, and s (@draw era_0@)igned a ten-year deal to buy nearly all its closed bodies from Fisher.

The price came from a fo (@draw formula@)rmula: Fisher's costs, plus seventeen point six percent.

    pause 0.3

    erase formula

B: Then in 1 (@draw event_rest@)926, three years before the d (@draw event_end era_1@)eal ran out, GM bought the rest of the company.

Why buy out a firm you already mostly own, with a contract already in place?
    highlight event_rest effect circle

    quiz "Why did GM buy the rest of Fisher Body three years early? Your guess:" judge false
        right: Hold on to that guess. Economists have argued over this one for decades.
        * Fisher was squeezing GM
        * To keep the Fisher brothers
        * Bodies got cheaper to make in-house
        * GM just wanted to be bigger

Coase's answer to why firm (@draw coase@)s exist at all was that using the market costs something. So which costs (@highlight event_rest effect underline@) tipped GM from buying to making?

    erase coase

B: Those are transaction costs: findin (@draw tcwords@)g a partner, haggling, writing the contract, and policing it.  (@draw williamson@)Oliver Williamson built a theory on them.
    draw tc

He won  (@highlight williamson@)a Nobel prize for it in 2009. The costliest ones, he argued (@draw sunk@), come after the deal is signed, when one side has sunk money it can't take back.

    pause 0.3

    erase williamson tc tcwords sunk

B: So: first a small ga (@draw r1@)me that shows the danger, then the text (@draw r2@)book story of GM and Fisher, then the people  (@draw r3@)who say it never happened.

## The hold-up game
vars:
    resale:
      value: 4
      min: 0
      max: 10
      step: 1
      label: Mould's resale value ($k)
use: game_tree
with:
    players:
      - Mould maker
      - Boatyard
    solve: true
    root:
      player: 0
      actions: [{label: Build, player: 1, actions: [{label: Pay 10, payoffs: [4, 6]}, {label: Squeeze, payoffs: [-2, 12]}]}, {label: Don't, payoffs: [0, 0]}]
bind_params:
    root.actions.0.actions.1.payoffs.0: round(resale) - 6
    root.actions.0.actions.1.payoffs.1: 16 - round(resale)
adjust:
    legend:
      scale: 1.3
      move: [0, -8]
    payoff_0_0:
      scale: 1.3
      move: [1.2, 0]
    payoff_0_1:
      scale: 1.3
      move: [1.2, 0]
    payoff_1:
      scale: 1.3
      move: [1.2, 0]

    scratch deal work ["cost 6","price 10","worth 16","$k, illustrative"] x 840 y 470 font_size 32 hidden true
    scratch scrap work ["specific asset","scrap value: 4"] x 840 y 470 font_size 32 hidden true
    text lost "10 lost" #b5482e x 840 y 330 font_size 44 hidden true

    card title "The hold-up game"

To see the danger, shrink GM down to Harbourtown. The boatyard wants a  (@draw legend@)new hull, and asks a local firm to build the mould for it.
    draw node_r

B: Illustrative numbers, i (@draw deal_box deal_line_1@)n thousands of dollars: the mould cos (@draw deal_line_2@)ts six to build, the boatyard agrees t (@draw deal_line_3 deal_line_4@)o pay ten, and the hull is worth sixteen to the yard.

If all goes to plan, the mould  (@draw edge_0_0 payoff_0_0@)maker makes four and the boatyard six.
    draw edge_0 node_0

    erase deal

But this mould fits only  (@draw scrap_box scrap_line_1@)this boatyard's hull. Anywhere else it's scrap, worth f (@draw scrap_line_2@)our. Economists call that a specific asset.

B: So once it's built, the ya (@draw edge_0_1 payoff_0_1@)rd can say: we'll pay four, not ten. Take it, or melt it down.

That's hold-up: once you've sunk the investment, the other side renegotiates.
    highlight edge_0_1 effect box

    ask "You're the boatyard: pay 10, or squeeze? Tap one." choose ["edge_0_0","edge_0_1"] judge false
        right: Now look at what each pays the yard.

B: Squeezing pays the yard twelve instead of six. So ste (@draw edge_1 payoff_1@)p back to the mould maker, before anything is built.
    highlight payoff_0_1 effect circle

    ask "You're the mould maker: build, or not? Tap one." choose ["edge_0","edge_1"] answer edge_1
        right: Don't: building now means a loss.
        wrong: Follow the squeeze back: building ends at minus two, not plus four. Here, don't.

It foresees the squeeze: minus tw (@draw solution@)o if it builds, zero if it doesn't. The mould is never made.

    erase scrap

B: And both lose. Ten thousand dollars (@draw lost@) of gains vanish, because a promise can't be trusted.

    erase lost

Now make the mould less specific. Drag its resale value, and find where the mould maker builds again.
    explore params ["resale"]

B: Once the mould could fetch what it cost somewhere else, the squeeze stops hurting, and it gets built. Specifici (@highlight payoff_0_1 effect circle@)ty is what gives the threat its bite.
    animate resale 6 duration 2

## Make it, or tie the knot
sources:
    - id: williamson1985
      title: The Economic Institutions of Capitalism
      authors: Oliver E. Williamson
      year: 1985
      finding: The more specific the assets in a transaction, the more it moves from spot markets to long-term contracts to unified ownership (vertical integration).
    - id: kca1978
      title: Vertical Integration, Appropriable Rents, and the Competitive Contracting Process
      authors: Benjamin Klein, Robert G. Crawford, Armen A. Alchian
      year: 1978
      finding: 'Journal of Law and Economics 21(2): GM–Fisher Body as hold-up. Under the 1919 cost-plus-17.6% exclusive contract, Fisher adopted an inefficient set-up and refused to locate body plants next to GM''s assembly plants as demand rose; GM bought the remaining 40% in 1926.'
    - id: joskow1987
      title: 'Contract Duration and Relationship-Specific Investments: Empirical Evidence from Coal Markets'
      authors: Paul L. Joskow
      year: 1987
      finding: 'American Economic Review 77(1): 168–185. In 277 coal contracts with electric utilities, contracts run longer where relationship-specific investments matter more; mine-mouth plants sign the longest.'
      url: https://econpapers.repec.org/RePEc:aea:aecrev:v:77:y:1987:i:1:p:168-85
    - id: ghm
      title: The Costs and Benefits of Ownership (Grossman & Hart, JPE 1986); Property Rights and the Nature of the Firm (Hart & Moore, JPE 1990)
      authors: Sanford J. Grossman, Oliver D. Hart, John Moore
      year: 1986
      finding: 'Ownership is residual control: the right to decide in contingencies the contract does not cover. It should go to the party whose relationship-specific investment matters most.'

    box b_buy "Buy" x 190 y 595 width 240 height 100 radius 10 font_size 42 hidden true
    box b_long "Long contract" x 500 y 595 width 300 height 100 radius 10 font_size 40 hidden true
    box b_make "Make" x 810 y 595 width 240 height 100 radius 10 font_size 42 style.stroke #2f6b8f hidden true
    text ex_buy "bolts" #8f887c x 190 y 515 font_size 30 hidden true
    text ex_long "tailor-made" #8f887c x 500 y 515 font_size 30 hidden true
    text ex_make "no one else could use" #8f887c x 810 y 515 font_size 30 hidden true
    arrow spec from.x 70 from.y 470 to.x 940 to.y 470 hidden true
    text spec_l "how specific?" x 830 y 440 font_size 30 hidden true
    source src cites ["kca1978"] x 240 y 290 of "Vertical Integration, Appropriable Rents, and the Competitive Contracting Process" hidden true
    text flint "Flint plant? No." #b5482e x 560 y 320 font_size 36 hidden true
    text markup "cost + 17.6%" #b5482e x 560 y 255 font_size 36 hidden true
    box gm "GM–Fisher, 1926" cites ["kca1978"] x 800 y 375 width 290 height 70 radius 8 font_size 30 hidden true
    box coal "mine-mouth power plant" cites ["joskow1987"] x 470 y 375 width 340 height 70 radius 8 font_size 30 hidden true
    text n277 "277 coal contracts" cites ["joskow1987"] x 500 y 290 font_size 36 hidden true
    text ghm_t "owner decides what no contract foresaw" #2f6b8f cites ["ghm"] x 500 y 270 font_size 36 hidden true
    cards mathq options [{"text":"Show me the math","goto":"math"},{"text":"Skip it","goto":"test"}] then test hidden true
    scratch m work [{"tex":"c = \\text{cost},\\; V = \\text{value},\\; R = \\text{resale}"},{"tex":"\\text{squeezed: build only if } R \\ge c"},{"tex":"\\text{worth building if } V \\ge c"},{"tex":"\\text{lost: } R < c \\le V"}] x 500 y 300 font_size 28 hidden true

    card title "Make it, or tie the knot"

So a spec (@draw spec@)ific investment invites hold-up, and the fear of hold-up kills good investments. Williamson's  (@draw spec_l@)cure: the more specific the asset, the less you leave to the market.

B: Standar (@draw b_buy ex_buy@)d parts, like bolts: just buy them. Something (@draw b_long ex_long@) tailor-made: sign a long contract. Something n (@draw b_make ex_make@)o one else could use: make it yourself.

Inside one firm, there's no one left to squeeze. The mould shop and the yard share one bottom line.
    highlight b_make effect box

B: In 1978 Benjamin  (@draw src@)Klein, Robert Crawford and Armen Alchian told GM and Fisher this way.

Car demand boomed in the mid-1920s, and GM wanted Fisher to  (@draw flint@)build a body plant beside its own plant in Flint.

B: Fisher refused. Under cost plus, a costly, out-of-the-wa (@draw markup@)y set-up paid more, since the seventeen point six percent rode on every extra dollar.

    erase flint markup

This time the supplier did the squeezing: GM was tied by its exclusive  (@draw gm@)contract. So in 1926 it made instead of bought.

    erase src

B: One story proves little. Paul Joskow looked at 27 (@draw n277@)7 contracts between coal mines and power plants.

Plants built right  (@draw coal@)at the mouth of a mine, with nowhere else to buy, signed m (@highlight b_long effect box@)uch longer contracts. Just what the theory predicts.

    erase n277

B: Sanford Grossman, Oliver Hart and John Moore added a twist: owning me (@draw ghm_t@)ans deciding whatever no contract foresaw. So give ownership to the side whose investment matters most.

    pause 0.3

    erase ghm_t

    hide gm coal

    draw mathq

    ask "Show the math, or skip it?" on mathq stage own
        intro: There's a one-line rule behind the hold-up game.

@math

    erase mathq

Call the mould's cost c, its value to the buyer V, and its resale value R.
    draw m_box m_line_1

Squeezed down to its resale value, the maker builds only if R is at least c.
    draw m_line_2

But for the two of them together, building pays whenever V is at least c.
    draw m_line_3

So every project with R below c, and c below V, is a good investment that hold-up kills.
    draw m_line_4

    erase m

@test

    erase mathq

    show gm coal

## Did it really happen?
sources:
    - id: coase2000
      title: The Acquisition of Fisher Body by General Motors
      authors: R. H. Coase
      year: 2000
      finding: 'Journal of Law and Economics 43(1): from the records, Fisher built eight body plants between 1922 and 1925 close to GM plants; the firms'' interests were closely aligned (Fisher brothers on GM''s board), so there is little sign Fisher held GM up.'
    - id: cms2000
      title: The Fable of Fisher Body
      authors: Ramon Casadesus-Masanell, Daniel F. Spulber
      year: 2000
      finding: 'Journal of Law and Economics 43(1): 67–104. GM held 60% from 1919; dealings showed trust, not opportunism; Fisher''s production technology did not exhibit asset specificity; the 1926 merger reflected coordination of production and inventories, supply assurance and access to the Fisher brothers'' executive talent.'
      url: https://econpapers.repec.org/RePEc:ucp:jlawec:v:43:y:2000:i:1:p:67-104
    - id: klein2000
      title: Fisher–General Motors and the Nature of the Firm
      authors: Benjamin Klein
      year: 2000
      finding: 'Journal of Law and Economics 43(1): 105–141. The contract broke down when GM''s demand for Fisher''s bodies rose unexpectedly and dramatically; vertical integration avoided the rigidity costs of the long-term contract.'
      url: https://econpapers.repec.org/RePEc:ucp:jlawec:v:43:y:2000:i:1:p:105-41
    - id: joskow1987
      title: 'Contract Duration and Relationship-Specific Investments: Empirical Evidence from Coal Markets'
      authors: Paul L. Joskow
      year: 1987
      finding: 'American Economic Review 77(1): longer contracts where relationship-specific investments matter more (277 coal contracts).'

    text h_story "Hold-up story" #b5482e x 250 y 625 font_size 44 hidden true
    text h_arch "The archives" #2f6b8f x 745 y 625 font_size 44 hidden true
    text c1 "won't build near GM" x 250 y 530 font_size 36 hidden true
    text c2 "assets tied to GM" x 250 y 430 font_size 36 hidden true
    text c3 "merger stops a squeeze" x 250 y 330 font_size 36 hidden true
    mark s1 around c1 kind strike hidden true
    text s2 "?" #b5482e x 455 y 430 font_size 44 hidden true
    text s3 "?" #b5482e x 455 y 330 font_size 44 hidden true
    text a1 "8 plants near GM, 1922–25" cites ["coase2000"] x 745 y 530 font_size 36 hidden true
    text a2 "nothing tied to GM alone" cites ["cms2000"] x 745 y 430 font_size 36 hidden true
    text a3 "coordination · the Fisher brothers" cites ["cms2000"] x 745 y 330 font_size 32 hidden true
    text coase "Ronald Coase, 2000" #8f887c cites ["coase2000"] x 745 y 580 font_size 28 hidden true
    text klein "Klein: demand jumped, contract too rigid" #8a5fa8 cites ["klein2000"] x 500 y 225 font_size 34 hidden true
    text conf1 "Hold-up shapes firms: mostly agreed" #2f6b8f cites ["joskow1987"] x 500 y 480 font_size 42 hidden true
    text conf2 "GM–Fisher: contested" #b5482e x 500 y 380 font_size 42 hidden true
    cards sortc check each hidden true
        Buy: printer paper
        Make or long contract: a die for one car's doors
        Buy: office cleaning
        Make or long contract: pipeline from one gas field
        Buy: tyres for a van
    text recap "Buy  →  Long contract  →  Make" x 500 y 250 font_size 40 hidden true
    text recap_l "the more specific the asset" #8f887c x 500 y 195 font_size 28 hidden true

    card title "Did it really happen?"

That's the textbook story. In 2000 Ronald Coase, then near (@draw coase h_arch@)ly ninety, went back to the records to check it.
    draw h_story c1 c2 c3

B: He found that Fisher buil (@draw a1@)t eight body plants between 1922 and 1925, cl (@draw s1@)ose to GM's.

    erase coase

Ramon Casadesus-Masanell and Daniel Spulber called theirs The Fable of Fisher B (@draw a2@)ody. They found nothing in Fisher's production tie (@draw s2@)d to GM alone.

B: Their reading: GM bought the rest t (@draw a3@)o coordinate production and supply, and to keep the Fishe (@draw s3@)r brothers as managers.

Klein answered in the same journal: the  (@draw klein@)contract broke down when GM's demand jumped, and the merger (@highlight klein@) ended a deal too rigid to adapt.

B: So the most famous example of hold-up may not be one. Awkward for the textbook. Not fatal for the theory.
    highlight h_story effect underline

    erase h_story h_arch c1 c2 c3 s1 s2 s3 a1 a2 a3 klein

How sure are we? That hold-up shapes contracts a (@draw conf1@)nd firms, backed by many studies like the coal contracts: mostly agreed. That G (@draw conf2@)M and Fisher show it: contested.

    pause 0.5

    hide conf1 conf2

    draw sortc

    ask "Buy, or make / tie up long? Sort each card." on sortc store f
        intro: Three new cases. First, sort these.
        right: All sorted: paper, cleaning and tyres you buy; the die and the pipeline you make or tie up long.
        wrong: {f} of {f.total} first time. Could anyone else use it? Here: buy paper, cleaning, tyres; the die and pipeline, make or tie up.

    erase sortc

    show conf1 conf2

    quiz "A firm spends a year building software that only one bank's systems can run. Which arrangement avoids hold-up?" voice b
        right: A long contract, or the bank making it in-house: once the code exists, it's worth nothing to anyone else.
        wrong: Ask who can squeeze whom once the software exists: here, a long contract, or the bank making it itself.
        + A long contract fixing the price, or the bank building it itself
        * A handshake, and settle the price later
        * Being paid only once it works

    quiz "Harbourtown's council and the boatyard plan a new dry dock. The yard's know-how decides how well it is used. Who should own it?"
        right: The boatyard: ownership is the right to decide what no contract foresaw, so it goes to the side whose investment matters most.
        wrong: Ownership is the right to decide what no contract foresaw. Give it to the side whose investment matters most: here, the boatyard.
        + The boatyard
        * The council
        * It doesn't matter

So, make or buy? Buy what (@draw recap recap_l@) anyone could supply. Make, or bind with a long contract, what only you can use. And check the archive bef (@highlight conf2 effect box@)ore you trust the famous example.

